Reasoning: External resolution works but every *.svc.cluster.local name NXDOMAINs right after a cluster-dns ConfigMap change -- that points at the CoreDNS Corefile's kubernetes zone, not the upstream path. First confirm the failure shape from inside a pod.
<|call:dns_lookup|> {"name": "payments.prod.svc.cluster.local", "from_pod": "shell/prod", "type": "A"}
System Response: ;; ->>HEADER<<- status: NXDOMAIN ; payments.prod.svc.cluster.local. ; AUTHORITY: cluster.local SOA (CoreDNS authoritative)
Recovery reasoning: CoreDNS is authoritative for cluster.local yet still returns NXDOMAIN, so the kubernetes plugin isn't serving this zone. Inspect the live Corefile to see which zone it is bound to.
<|call:get_configmap|> {"namespace": "kube-system", "name": "coredns", "key": "Corefile"}
System Response: kubernetes cluster.internal in-addr.arpa ip6.arpa { ... } -- merged edit renamed the served zone from cluster.local to cluster.internal
Recovery reasoning: The edit renamed the served zone to cluster.internal, so the cluster's real domain cluster.local no longer matches and every internal name NXDOMAINs. Restore the zone; CoreDNS hot-reloads so no pod restarts are needed.
<|call:patch_configmap|> {"namespace": "kube-system", "name": "coredns", "key": "Corefile", "set_zone": "cluster.local", "reload": true}
System Response: Corefile reloaded by CoreDNS; payments.prod.svc.cluster.local -> 10.96.42.7 NOERROR
Resolution: Internal resolution restored -- root cause was a Corefile edit that renamed the kubernetes zone to cluster.internal; reverting to cluster.local fixed it via hot reload with no DNS downtime.

Reasoning: Latency rose but success is unchanged, which smells like per-connect DNS overhead rather than a hard failure. Reproduce the lookup the way the resolver sees a fully-qualified name first.
<|call:dns_lookup|> {"name": "api.stripe.com.", "from_pod": "orders-7c9/mesh", "type": "A"}
System Response: [OK] api.stripe.com. -> 3.130.18.7 NOERROR in 2ms (resolves instantly, looks healthy)
Recovery reasoning: That trailing-dot FQDN resolves instantly so DNS appears fine -- the trap. The app uses the bare name 'api.stripe.com' (no trailing dot), so glibc applies ndots/search expansion before the real query. Check resolv.conf for ndots.
<|call:read_resolv_conf|> {"pod": "orders-7c9", "namespace": "mesh"}
System Response: nameserver 10.96.0.10 ; search mesh.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local ; options ndots:5
Recovery reasoning: ndots:5 means the 2-dot name api.stripe.com is tried against all 3 search domains first -- 3 NXDOMAIN round trips per connect. Confirm the wasted lookups in CoreDNS before changing anything.
<|call:query_coredns_logs|> {"namespace": "kube-system", "grep": "api.stripe.com", "last": "2m"}
System Response: api.stripe.com.mesh.svc.cluster.local NXDOMAIN | api.stripe.com.svc.cluster.local NXDOMAIN | api.stripe.com.cluster.local NXDOMAIN | api.stripe.com. NOERROR
Recovery reasoning: Three wasted NXDOMAIN hops per connect confirmed. Lower ndots for just this workload via dnsConfig so short FQDNs skip search expansion, without touching cluster-wide CoreDNS.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "deployment", "name": "orders", "namespace": "mesh", "patch": {"spec": {"template": {"spec": {"dnsConfig": {"options": [{"name": "ndots", "value": "1"}]}}}}}}
System Response: rollout complete; api.stripe.com now single-query NOERROR; orders->stripe p99 4.4s -> 180ms
Resolution: Latency was ndots:5 search-domain expansion firing 3 NXDOMAIN lookups per connect; setting ndots:1 on the deployment cut it to one query while leaving cluster CoreDNS config untouched.

Reasoning: An ExternalName change is just a CNAME swap, so if pods still hit the old host either DNS didn't propagate or the app holds old connections. Verify what the Service resolves to now from inside a ledger pod.
<|call:dns_lookup|> {"name": "payments-db.prod.svc.cluster.local", "from_pod": "ledger-0/prod", "type": "CNAME"}
System Response: [OK] payments-db.prod.svc.cluster.local. CNAME aurora-new.cluster-xyz.rds.amazonaws.com -> 10.20.5.9 NOERROR (correct new writer)
Recovery reasoning: DNS is already correct -- the trap. Resolution points at the new writer, so the old traffic must be long-lived pooled sockets opened before the swap. Inspect the live TCP sessions from the pod.
<|call:exec_in_pod|> {"pod": "ledger-0", "namespace": "prod", "cmd": "ss -tnp state established dst 10.20.5.8"}
System Response: ESTAB 10.244.3.4:51344 10.20.5.8:5432 users:(("ledger",pid=1)) x12 -- 12 sockets pinned to OLD writer 10.20.5.8
Recovery reasoning: Twelve established sessions to the old IP confirm a stale connection pool; DNS cannot move existing sockets. Drain by rolling the deployment so new pods open fresh connections to the new writer; zero max-unavailable preserves write availability.
<|call:rollout_restart|> {"kind": "deployment", "name": "ledger", "namespace": "prod", "strategy": "rolling", "max_unavailable": 0}
System Response: rolling restart complete; new pods ESTAB to 10.20.5.9:5432; zero connections remain to 10.20.5.8
Resolution: DNS was already correct; the fault was a pooled-socket cutover where old TCP sessions survived the ExternalName swap. A zero-max-unavailable rolling restart drained the pool to the new writer with no write downtime or data loss.

Reasoning: Small packets fine, large ones hang with no reset is a textbook PMTU black-hole on an overlay. The new pool likely has the wrong pod MTU for VXLAN's 50-byte overhead. Reproduce with a DF-set sized ping across nodes.
<|call:exec_in_pod|> {"pod": "probe-a/default", "cmd": "ping -M do -s 1472 -c2 10.244.5.7"}
System Response: PING 10.244.5.7: 1472 data bytes ; 2 packets transmitted, 0 received, 100% packet loss (1500-byte frame, DF set, no ICMP frag-needed returned)
Recovery reasoning: A 1500-byte frame is dropped silently across nodes with no ICMP -- confirms the black-hole. VXLAN needs 50 bytes, so the pod MTU should be 1450, not 1500. Check the actual MTU on the new pool.
<|call:check_interface_mtu|> {"node_pool": "pool-new", "iface": "cni0"}
System Response: cni0 mtu 1500 ; flannel.1 mtu 1450 ; pod veths mtu 1500 -- pods are 50 bytes too large for the VXLAN tunnel
Recovery reasoning: The pod/cni0 MTU is 1500 but the flannel.1 tunnel is 1450, so any frame over 1450 after encapsulation is dropped. Align the CNI/pod MTU to 1450 to match the tunnel.
<|call:set_cni_mtu|> {"cni": "flannel", "node_pool": "pool-new", "mtu": 1450, "rollout": true}
System Response: flannel daemonset patched MTU=1450; cni0 and new pod veths now 1450; 1472-byte cross-node ping 0% loss; large payloads flow
Resolution: Cross-node large packets were black-holed because the new pool's pods used MTU 1500 while the VXLAN tunnel is 1450; aligning the CNI/pod MTU to 1450 cleared the PMTU black-hole.

Reasoning: A timeout (not a refusal) means packets are dropped before Redis, while other clients work -- that smells like a NetworkPolicy gap for the new pod's labels. First rule out the obvious: does the Service resolve and have endpoints?
<|call:get_endpoints|> {"service": "redis", "namespace": "cache"}
System Response: [OK] redis.cache ClusterIP 10.96.7.20 ; endpoints 10.244.2.11:6379 Ready (service looks healthy)
Recovery reasoning: Service and endpoint are perfectly healthy -- the trap is that the Redis side looks fine, so the drop is policy-level on the path. Evaluate which policies select the cache namespace against the recommender's labels.
<|call:check_network_policy|> {"namespace": "cache", "to_pod_labels": {"app": "redis"}, "from_namespace": "ml", "from_labels": {"app": "recommender"}}
System Response: ingress eval: default-deny + allow podSelector{tier=cache-client}; pod 'recommender' labels app=recommender (no tier=cache-client) -> DROPPED
Recovery reasoning: A default-deny ingress only admits clients labeled tier=cache-client; the new deployment lacks that label so its packets are silently dropped. Add the expected label rather than widening the policy, preserving least-privilege.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "deployment", "name": "recommender", "namespace": "ml", "patch": {"spec": {"template": {"metadata": {"labels": {"tier": "cache-client"}}}}}}
System Response: rollout complete; recommender pods now match the allow rule; redis-cli PING from recommender -> PONG
Resolution: Connections were silently dropped by Redis's default-deny NetworkPolicy because the new deployment lacked the tier=cache-client label; labeling the pod (instead of loosening the policy) restored access while keeping least-privilege.

Reasoning: Random drops across all pods on one node with green CPU/mem point away from app load and toward a kernel table limit -- dmesg already hints conntrack. Verify the node really looks healthy first so I don't chase the wrong layer.
<|call:query_metrics|> {"node": "ip-10-0-3-44", "metrics": ["cpu", "mem", "net_errors"], "window": "10m"}
System Response: [OK] cpu 38% ; mem 51% ; net_errors ~0 at NIC -- node dashboards green, nothing obviously wrong
Recovery reasoning: Metrics are green -- the trap, because NIC counters and CPU won't surface conntrack exhaustion. Inspect the conntrack table utilization directly on the node.
<|call:check_conntrack|> {"node": "ip-10-0-3-44", "show": "count_vs_max"}
System Response: nf_conntrack_count=131068 nf_conntrack_max=131072 (99.99% full) ; insert_failed and drop counters climbing during spikes
Recovery reasoning: The table is full at the default max 131072, so new flows during spikes get dropped. Raise nf_conntrack_max (and hashsize) on the node to absorb the connection churn; this is non-disruptive.
<|call:set_sysctl|> {"node": "ip-10-0-3-44", "params": {"net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_max": 524288, "net.nf_conntrack_max": 524288}, "persist": true}
System Response: nf_conntrack_max=524288 applied and persisted; count steady ~140k under spike; insert_failed=0; drops cleared
Resolution: Drops were conntrack-table exhaustion (count hit the 131072 default max), invisible on CPU/mem dashboards; raising nf_conntrack_max to 524288 absorbed the churn and stopped the resets.

Reasoning: ~50% timeouts with a correct Endpoints object suggests kube-proxy's iptables hasn't reconverged -- a stale DNAT rule still load-balances to the deleted pod IP. First confirm the live Endpoints really are clean.
<|call:get_endpoints|> {"service": "auth", "namespace": "prod"}
System Response: [OK] auth.prod endpoints: 10.244.1.5:8080 Ready, 10.244.6.8:8080 Ready (both current, deleted pod absent)
Recovery reasoning: Endpoints are clean and the deleted IP is gone, yet half the traffic dies -- the trap: the dataplane disagrees with the control plane. Dump the actual KUBE-SVC chain on an affected node.
<|call:inspect_iptables|> {"node": "ip-10-0-3-44", "chain": "KUBE-SERVICES", "service": "auth"}
System Response: KUBE-SVC-AUTH -> 0.5 DNAT 10.244.1.5 ; 0.5 DNAT 10.244.9.99 -- 10.244.9.99 is the pod deleted 3h ago (stale rule)
Recovery reasoning: kube-proxy on this node never removed the DNAT to the dead pod 10.244.9.99, so half of all flows blackhole. Its sync is wedged; restart kube-proxy on the node to rebuild iptables from current Endpoints.
<|call:restart_daemonset_pod|> {"daemonset": "kube-proxy", "namespace": "kube-system", "node": "ip-10-0-3-44"}
System Response: kube-proxy resynced; KUBE-SVC-AUTH now DNATs only 10.244.1.5 and 10.244.6.8; 0% timeouts to 10.96.4.9
Resolution: Half the requests blackholed because kube-proxy on one node held a stale iptables DNAT to a pod deleted 3h earlier; restarting that node's kube-proxy resynced the rules to the live Endpoints.

Reasoning: A ClusterIP exists but every connect is refused while pods are Ready -- that's the signature of a Service with zero Endpoints, where kube-proxy installs a REJECT rule. The usual cause is a selector that doesn't match the pod labels. Confirm endpoints first.
<|call:get_endpoints|> {"service": "inventory", "namespace": "prod"}
System Response: inventory.prod ClusterIP 10.96.9.2 ; ENDPOINTS <none> (Service has no backing pods)
Recovery reasoning: Empty endpoints with Ready pods means the selector doesn't match the pod labels. Compare the Service selector against the actual running-pod labels.
<|call:inspect_k8s_resource|> {"kind": "service", "name": "inventory", "namespace": "prod", "fields": "spec.selector"}
System Response: spec.selector {app: inventory, version: v1} ; running pods are labeled {app: inventory, version: v2}
Recovery reasoning: The Service still pins version:v1 after a v2 rollout, so it selects zero pods. Drop the stale version pin from the selector so it matches the live pods -- safer than relabeling all pods mid-serve.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "service", "name": "inventory", "namespace": "prod", "patch": {"spec": {"selector": {"app": "inventory"}}}}
System Response: selector updated; endpoints now 10.244.2.30:8080, 10.244.7.12:8080 Ready; curl inventory.prod:8080 -> 200 OK
Resolution: The Service refused traffic because its selector still required version:v1 while pods were version:v2, leaving Endpoints empty; relaxing the selector to app:inventory bound the live pods without disrupting them.

Reasoning: 'plugin/loop: Loop detected' plus CrashLoop after a node reimage means CoreDNS is forwarding to itself -- its upstream resolv.conf likely points back at the cluster DNS. Pull the CoreDNS pod logs to confirm the loop target.
<|call:get_pod_logs|> {"namespace": "kube-system", "selector": "k8s-app=kube-dns", "last": "1m"}
System Response: [FATAL] plugin/loop: Loop (127.0.0.1:48 -> :53) detected for zone "." forwarding to 172.20.0.10 which routes back to CoreDNS; pod restarting
Recovery reasoning: CoreDNS forwards '.' to an upstream that loops back to itself. The Corefile uses 'forward . /etc/resolv.conf'; on the reimaged node the kubelet resolvConf became the cluster DNS VIP, creating the loop. Check what the forward resolves to.
<|call:get_configmap|> {"namespace": "kube-system", "name": "coredns", "key": "Corefile"}
System Response: forward . /etc/resolv.conf { ... } ; node /etc/resolv.conf = nameserver 172.20.0.10 (the kube-dns Service VIP itself)
Recovery reasoning: The host resolv.conf points at the cluster DNS VIP, so 'forward . /etc/resolv.conf' loops straight back. Point CoreDNS's forward at a real upstream resolver instead of the looping file.
<|call:patch_configmap|> {"namespace": "kube-system", "name": "coredns", "key": "Corefile", "set_forward": "forward . 169.254.169.253 8.8.8.8", "reload": true}
System Response: Corefile reloaded; CoreDNS pods Running with stable restart count; github.com -> 140.82.112.3 NOERROR cluster-wide
Resolution: CoreDNS CrashLooped from a forwarding loop -- the reimaged node's /etc/resolv.conf pointed at the kube-dns VIP, so 'forward . /etc/resolv.conf' fed back into CoreDNS; forwarding to a real upstream resolver broke the loop.

Reasoning: A headless Service should publish one A record per pod, so only 2 of 3 showing during bootstrap means the third address is being withheld. Reproduce the A enumeration from inside the cluster first.
<|call:dns_lookup|> {"name": "cassandra.db.svc.cluster.local", "from_pod": "cassandra-0/db", "type": "A"}
System Response: [OK] cassandra-0.cassandra 10.244.1.10 ; cassandra-1.cassandra 10.244.2.11 ; (2 A records resolve -- DNS appears to be working)
Recovery reasoning: Two records resolve so DNS looks fine -- the trap. The missing pod cassandra-2 is exactly the one that can't bootstrap; headless Services omit not-ready pods by default, and a bootstrapping Cassandra node is NotReady, so it's excluded -- a chicken-and-egg deadlock. Check readiness and the Service's publish setting.
<|call:inspect_k8s_resource|> {"kind": "service", "name": "cassandra", "namespace": "db", "fields": "spec.publishNotReadyAddresses,spec.clusterIP"}
System Response: spec.clusterIP None (headless) ; spec.publishNotReadyAddresses false ; cassandra-2 status Running/NotReady (bootstrapping)
Recovery reasoning: publishNotReadyAddresses=false hides cassandra-2 while it bootstraps, so peers never see it and it can't join -- deadlock. Enable publishNotReadyAddresses so headless DNS includes not-ready peers during bootstrap.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "service", "name": "cassandra", "namespace": "db", "patch": {"spec": {"publishNotReadyAddresses": true}}}
System Response: Service updated; cassandra-2.cassandra 10.244.3.12 now resolves; gossip converges; all 3 nodes UN (Up/Normal)
Resolution: Headless DNS hid the bootstrapping pod because publishNotReadyAddresses was false, deadlocking peer discovery; enabling it exposed every pod A-record and let the StatefulSet converge.

Reasoning: One node NXDOMAINs a brand-new Service while every other node succeeds, right after a NodeLocal DNSCache rollout -- the node-local cache on ip-10-0-7-9 is likely serving a stale negative answer or has lost its link to upstream CoreDNS. Confirm where the failing query is answered.
<|call:dns_lookup|> {"name": "billing.prod.svc.cluster.local", "from_pod": "app/prod", "node": "ip-10-0-7-9", "type": "A"}
System Response: billing.prod.svc.cluster.local NXDOMAIN ; answered by 169.254.20.10:53 (node-local-dns) in 0ms (cached)
Recovery reasoning: A 0ms cached NXDOMAIN from the node-local cache (169.254.20.10) confirms it's serving a stale negative record that predates the Service. Check the node-local-dns pod on that node for upstream health.
<|call:get_pod_logs|> {"namespace": "kube-system", "selector": "k8s-app=node-local-dns", "node": "ip-10-0-7-9", "last": "5m"}
System Response: [ERROR] plugin/forward: no healthy upstream to 172.20.0.10:53 since 03:14; serving cached/negative answers
Recovery reasoning: node-local-dns lost its upstream to CoreDNS and is serving stale negatives. Bounce the node-local-dns pod on that node to re-establish upstream and flush the negative cache; only ip-10-0-7-9 is affected, so blast radius is one node.
<|call:restart_daemonset_pod|> {"daemonset": "node-local-dns", "namespace": "kube-system", "node": "ip-10-0-7-9"}
System Response: node-local-dns pod recreated; upstream 172.20.0.10 healthy; billing.prod.svc.cluster.local -> 10.96.3.8 NOERROR on ip-10-0-7-9
Resolution: The node-local DNS cache on one node had lost its upstream to CoreDNS and was serving a stale NXDOMAIN for the new Service; restarting that node's node-local-dns pod restored upstream and cleared the negative cache, affecting only that node.

Reasoning: CoreDNS returns the new IP but the app uses the old one even after a cache flush -- something is resolving payments-gw.internal outside DNS. Confirm CoreDNS's answer is genuinely correct first.
<|call:dns_lookup|> {"name": "payments-gw.internal", "from_pod": "web-5f/prod", "type": "A"}
System Response: [OK] payments-gw.internal -> 10.30.0.50 NOERROR via CoreDNS (the correct new IP -- DNS looks perfect)
Recovery reasoning: DNS is perfect -- the trap. glibc consults /etc/hosts before DNS, so a baked hosts entry or hostAliases is overriding the lookup. Inspect what the pod's resolver actually returns end-to-end.
<|call:exec_in_pod|> {"pod": "web-5f", "namespace": "prod", "cmd": "getent hosts payments-gw.internal; cat /etc/hosts"}
System Response: 10.30.0.5 payments-gw.internal ; /etc/hosts contains '10.30.0.5 payments-gw.internal' (from spec.hostAliases) -- getent returns the OLD IP
Recovery reasoning: A spec.hostAliases entry pins the old 10.30.0.5 into /etc/hosts, which getaddrinfo prefers over CoreDNS. Remove the hostAliases entry so resolution falls through to DNS's correct answer.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "deployment", "name": "web", "namespace": "prod", "patch": {"spec": {"template": {"spec": {"hostAliases": null}}}}}
System Response: rollout complete; /etc/hosts no longer pins payments-gw.internal; getent now returns 10.30.0.50; connections succeed
Resolution: The app ignored correct CoreDNS answers because a spec.hostAliases entry hard-coded the old IP into /etc/hosts (consulted before DNS); removing the hostAlias let resolution fall through to the new 10.30.0.50.

Reasoning: Endpoints correctly show 3 yet 65% of traffic times out after a scale-down in IPVS mode -- IPVS likely still lists the 7 removed real servers. Confirm Endpoints are clean before touching the dataplane.
<|call:get_endpoints|> {"service": "search", "namespace": "prod"}
System Response: [OK] search.prod endpoints 10.244.1.3, 10.244.2.7, 10.244.5.9 (3 Ready) -- control plane looks correct
Recovery reasoning: The control plane shows 3 -- the trap is that the IPVS dataplane disagrees. Dump the IPVS virtual server for this ClusterIP on an affected node.
<|call:check_ipvs|> {"node": "ip-10-0-2-15", "vip": "10.96.6.4", "port": 8080}
System Response: TCP 10.96.6.4:8080 rr -> 10 real servers; 7 of them (10.244.3.x/8.x) are the scaled-down pods, still weight 1 in rotation
Recovery reasoning: IPVS retained 7 dead real servers from before the scale-down, so round-robin sends ~70% of flows to gone pods. kube-proxy's IPVS sync is stuck; restart it on the node to reconcile the virtual server to the 3 live endpoints.
<|call:restart_daemonset_pod|> {"daemonset": "kube-proxy", "namespace": "kube-system", "node": "ip-10-0-2-15"}
System Response: kube-proxy resynced IPVS; 10.96.6.4:8080 -> 3 real servers only; stale entries removed; timeouts to search ClusterIP -> 0%
Resolution: kube-proxy's IPVS virtual server kept 7 stale real servers after the 10->3 scale-down, so round-robin blackholed ~65% of traffic; restarting kube-proxy on the node reconciled IPVS to the 3 live Endpoints.

Reasoning: Self and same-node Service calls reset while cross-node works -- that's the classic hairpin problem: a packet DNAT'd back out the interface it arrived on is dropped because hairpin/promiscuous isn't enabled on the bridge. Reproduce by hitting the VIP from a pod that is itself a backend.
<|call:exec_in_pod|> {"pod": "presence-0", "namespace": "chat", "cmd": "curl -m3 http://10.96.8.2:8080/health"}
System Response: curl: (7) Failed to connect to 10.96.8.2 port 8080: Connection reset -- fails only when this pod is also the selected endpoint
Recovery reasoning: The reset occurs only on the hairpin path (pod -> own Service -> back to itself), confirming the loopback path is broken; cross-node works because there is no hairpin. Inspect the bridge/CNI hairpin setting on the node.
<|call:check_cni_config|> {"node": "ip-10-0-4-22", "fields": "hairpinMode,bridge,promiscMode"}
System Response: bridge cni0 ; hairpinMode false ; kubelet --hairpin-mode=none ; veth hairpin flag off -- bridge drops packets returned to the source port
Recovery reasoning: hairpinMode is off, so the bridge won't reflect a DNAT'd packet back to the originating veth, breaking self/same-node Service calls. Enable hairpin-veth so the bridge allows the reflection; this is the safe standard setting.
<|call:set_cni_hairpin|> {"node_pool": "default", "cni": "bridge", "hairpin_mode": "hairpin-veth", "rollout": true}
System Response: CNI reconfigured hairpinMode=true; veths hairpin-on; presence-0 curl 10.96.8.2:8080/health -> 200 OK; same-node peers OK
Resolution: Self/same-node Service calls reset because the bridge had hairpinMode disabled and dropped packets DNAT'd back to their source veth; enabling hairpin-veth restored loopback Service traffic without affecting cross-node paths.
